  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "A University of Tampa historian draws parallels between the growing American backlash against Flock Safety's ALPR cameras \u2014 including contract cancellations, state permit revocations, and vandalism in at least 36 states \u2014 and colonial resistance to British rule culminating in the Boston Tea Party. The piece cites misuse incidents, ICE data-sharing lawsuits, and Flock CEO Garett Langley's appeals to safety-vs-liberty tradeoffs.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "In at least 36 states, opponents of the technology have taken matters into their own hands, destroying or impairing ALPRs rather than wait for local governments to step in.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "In September 2026, Florida's Department of Transportation revoked permits for ALPRs on its highway system.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "Langley suggested Americans should again prioritize safety and hoped Americans would be willing to 'compromise' on issues of privacy.",
      "paragraph_idx": 26
    }
  ],
